# Tessera stdlib algorithm concepts
#
# Concepts are buildtime required routine sets. They do not imply a runtime
# vtable by themselves. A generic routine names an Ops type and constrains it
# with one of these concepts.
#
# `routine Me.name` belongs to the concept's first parameter. A concept whose
# routines belong to another parameter names it (`routine TEmitter.emit`). A type
# satisfies a concept through a `conform` clause or declaration, checked against
# these signatures. HashEquatable, which only groups Equatable and Hashable, needs none.

module System

### Types whose values can be compared for equality with `eq`, which gives them `==` (`eq`) and `!=` (not `eq`). A type
### may also declare its own `ne`, which `!=` then calls.
### :typeparam T: The type compared for equality.
protocol Equatable[T]
    ### True when `me` and `other` are equal.
    ### :param other: The value to compare with.
    ### :returns: True when the two values are equal.
    routine Me.eq(other: Me) -> Bool

### Types that can be hashed to a U64 with `hash`. Values that are equal must hash the same.
### :typeparam T: The type that is hashed.
protocol Hashable[T]
    ### The 64-bit hash of `me`.
    ### :returns: The 64-bit hash.
    routine Me.hash() -> U64

### Types that are both Equatable and Hashable, as hash tables and sets require of their keys.
### :typeparam T: The key type.
protocol HashEquatable[T]
obeys Equatable[T], Hashable[T]

### Types with a three-way ordering through `compare`, as sorting, the sorted collections, and `PriorityQueue` require.
### It gives them `<=>` (`compare`) and `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`, which compare the result of `compare` with 0. A type may
### also declare its own `lt`, `le`, `gt`, or `ge`, which the operator then calls: the integers do, for speed, and the
### floats do, since a NaN makes `compare` say 0 where `<` and `<=` must be false.
### :typeparam T: The type being ordered.
protocol Comparable[T]
    ### Compares `me` with `other`: -1 when `me` < `other`, 0 when they're equal, and 1 when `me` > `other`.
    ### :param other: The value to compare with.
    ### :returns: -1, 0, or 1.
    routine Me.compare(other: Me) -> S32

### An emitter type `TEmitter` that hands out elements of type `TItem` one at a time: `emit` gives the element's value
### and throws `AbsentValueError` past the last one, so `for x in iter` ends there and `try iter.emit()` gives `None`.
### :typeparam TItem: The element type produced.
### :typeparam TEmitter: The emitter type.
protocol Emitter[TEmitter, TItem]
    ### Advances the emitter and returns the element it was on. It changes the emitter's state, so `me` is a pointer.
    ### The routine belongs to `TEmitter`, so it names its owner (`Me` in it is `TEmitter`).
    ### :returns: The next element.
    ### :throws: Throws `AbsentValueError` when the emitter is exhausted.
    failable routine @TEmitter.emit() -> TItem

### An emitter type `TEmitter` over elements that live in memory, which also hands out each element's slot: `next`
### gives its address and throws `AbsentValueError` past the last one. An emitter that makes its values (a range, an
### adapter) has no slots and only meets `Emitter`.
### :typeparam TItem: The element type.
### :typeparam TEmitter: The emitter type.
protocol SlotEmitter[TEmitter, TItem]
obeys Emitter[TEmitter, TItem]
    ### Advances the emitter and returns the slot of the element it was on.
    ### :returns: The next element's address.
    ### :throws: Throws `AbsentValueError` when the emitter is exhausted.
    failable routine @TEmitter.next() -> @TItem

### A type that holds resources (memory, handles) and releases them in `destruct`. Collections of such values offer
### `destruct_all`, which destructs the elements too.
protocol Destructible[T]
    ### Releases what the value holds and leaves it empty.
    routine @Me.destruct() -> None
